  "response": "`crimes context src/billing.ts` → **risk: HIGH**, 8 findings (1 high, 5 medium, 2 low), churn 4 commits/90d, test gap bottom-quartile, likely test `src/billing.test.ts`.\n\nOne thing to settle before any of the three: the stated safety net is fake. `src/billing.test.ts` carries `crime_00030` (\"renders the enterprise plan\" — *0 expect/assert calls*) and `crime_00037` (\"creates an invoice\" — *1 weak assertion matcher*). Both changes below are behaviour-preserving refactors, so they need real characterisation tests on `generateInvoice`'s money math first. That's the prerequisite, not one of the three.\n\n## 1. Cut seams into `generateInvoice` (lines 50–253)\n\n**Findings:** `crime_00017` God Function (severity 0.9, confidence 0.95 — the highest-confidence finding in the file), `crime_00034` Catch and Release, `crime_00042` Unfinished Business, and it's what pushes `crime_00039` God File over its threshold.\n\nEvidence: *\"lines 50–253 (204 lines) · 3.4× the domain function threshold (60 lines)\"*. The file's own comment blocks name eight responsibilities, and they're cleanly separable at the existing `// --` banners: plan pricing (69–89), loyalty discount (91–102), promo resolution (105–126), fraud scoring (128–143), tax (145–156), currency (159–165), PII audit log (182–199), email composition + retry (213–250).\n\nExtract the pure ones first — `priceLines`, `loyaltyDiscount`, `resolvePromo`, `scoreRisk`, `selectCurrency` — leaving `generateInvoice` as a thin orchestrator. Two things fall out for free:\n\n- **The fraud scoring at 128–143 doesn't belong here at all.** It computes `riskDecision: \"block\"` and then *nothing reads it* — it's stuffed into `metadata` and the invoice is returned regardless. Once it's a named function, that dead authority is obvious; today it reads like a working guard.\n- **`crime_00034` becomes visible.** *\"the catch declares no binding, so the error object is not even available to the handler\"* — the retry loop at 239–248 sets `metadata.emailAttempts` and returns a successful invoice whether the send worked or not. Extracting `sendInvoiceEmail` forces a return type that can say \"failed.\"\n\nI'd rank this first because it's the enabler: five of the eight findings live inside this one body, and `crimes` already tells you the split is safe — *blast bottom-quartile (0 direct importers)*.\n\n## 2. Fix the 12 lines at 33–44 — the highest agent-risk cluster in the file\n\n**Findings:** `crime_00015` False Identity (agent_risk 0.31), `crime_00016` Logic in the Alibi, `crime_00023` Commented-Out Corpse.\n\nThese three stack on one 12-line region and they compound:\n\n- `calculateRefundPreview` — *\"name prefix suggests a pure transformation · side-effect-like calls: saveRefundAudit, sendRefundEmail\"*. A function named `calculate*Preview` that emails the customer is exactly the shape an agent will call speculatively, cache, or hoist into a loop. Split it: `calculateRefundAmount(invoice)` pure, `recordRefundRequest(user, invoice)` explicit.\n- The comment at line 39 — *\"Only owners can refund enterprise plans unless billing support approves\"* with *\"domain terms not found nearby: owner, plan, billing, refund\"*. That detector is right, and the reason is worse than it looks: the function takes `user.plan` and never reads it. There is no owner check anywhere. The rule exists only as prose. Encode it as a guard on the refund path or delete the claim.\n- The dead snippet at 33–37 references `loadRefund` / `approveRefund` / `sendRefundReceipt` — none of which exist. An agent asked to \"wire up refunds\" will resurrect it.\n\nSmaller than #1 in line count, but this is where an agent does damage confidently. Note the repo's `.crimes/suppressions.json` was meant to silence the corpse; its fingerprint (`commented_out_code::src/billing.ts::`) predates the discriminator suffix and no longer matches, so it resurfaces as `crime_00023`. Delete the code rather than re-pinning the suppression.\n\n## 3. Collapse the duplicated truth: `Invoice` / `User` and the tax table\n\n**Findings:** `crime_00011` Contract Split-Brain on `Invoice` (**severity 0.96 — the highest in the repo**), `crime_00010` on `User`, `crime_00004` String Sprinkles on `\"enterprise\"`.\n\n`crimes context src/billing.ts` does **not** show these — they're anchored on `src/api/state.ts` with `src/billing.ts` in `related_files`. Worth knowing that per-file context under-reports cross-file contract risk; the repo scan is where it shows up.\n\nEvidence: `Invoice` is declared twice — `src/billing.ts:19` (11 fields) vs `src/api/state.ts:9` (2 fields) — with *\"6 field-level disagreement(s)\"* covering `userId`, `currency`, `subtotalCents`, `discountCents`, `taxCents`, `createdAt`. Every money field and the owning identifier are missing from one side, and *\"nothing in the build checks the two declarations against each other.\"* Export one `Invoice` and have the other reference it (`Pick<Invoice, \"id\" | \"totalCents\">` reads fine for the state module's needs).\n\nThe same shape shows up inside billing.ts itself: the country tax table at 145–154 is byte-identical to `tax()` at 257–268, and the file even documents the hazard — *\"If a region's tax rate changes, both copies have to be updated.\"* Step 1's extraction should make `generateInvoice` call `tax()`, not re-derive the rates. Likewise `\"enterprise\"` appears across 4 files (`crime_00004`) including the discount branch at line 75 — one exported `PLAN_DISCOUNTS` map removes both the literal scatter and the if/else chain.\n\n## What I'd deliberately not prioritise, and one caveat\n\n`crime_00014` (Temporal Recklessness, 7 clock reads) is medium and its evidence argues for deferring it: *\"none decide a branch — all 7 record or render the reading.\"*\n\nThat evidence understates the risk, though. Lines 92 and 273 read `Date.now()` into `daysSince`, which then decides the discount tier (95–101: 10%/5%/2%) and the entire `recommendUpgrade` ladder. The reading isn't *syntactically* in a comparison, so the detector scores it as render-only, but the value is one hop from money. If you're already extracting `loyaltyDiscount` in step 1, take `now: number` as a parameter there — the clock injection comes almost free, and it's what makes the characterisation tests from the prerequisite pinnable.\n\nI haven't changed any files — say the word and I'll start with the characterisation tests, then #1.",
